{:edb/db-before-t 1237 :edb/db-after-t 1238 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534550 50 #inst "2026-10-08T00:35:49.378Z" 13194139534550 true] [17592186064198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294538 13194139534550 true] [17592186078360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294483 13194139534550 true] [17592186083216 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294531 13194139534550 true] [17592186087717 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294835 13194139534550 true] [17592186087818 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294674 13194139534550 true] [17592186087826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294828 13194139534550 true] [17592186092178 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294524 13194139534550 true] [17592186096728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294469 13194139534550 true] [17592186097288 1100 "Given that  ${a}_{n}={n}^{2}-5$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\le 0$ for all $n\\ge 1$\n- ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\n- ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139534550 true] [17592186097288 1100 "Given that $a_{n} = n^{2} - 5$ for $n \\ge 1$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{n + 1} - a_{n} \\le 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$\n2. $a_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\n3. $a_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139534550 false] [17592186097288 1145 "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is defined for $n\\ge 1.$ To determine whether ${a}_{n}$ is increasing or decreasing, we can use the following test:\n\n- If a sequence ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* then for all $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}\\ge {a}_{n}\\,⟹\\,{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n- If a sequence ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing,* then for all $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}\\le {a}_{n}\\,⟹\\,{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\le 0.\n$$\n\n- If neither of these conditions holds, the sequence is *neither increasing nor decreasing*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the difference ${a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n} & =(n+1)^{2}-5-({n}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =(n+1)^{2}-5-{n}^{2}+5 \\\\  & ={n}^{2}+2n+1-{n}^{2} \\\\  & =2n+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *positive* for $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTo see this concretely, set ${b}_{n}=2n+1.$ Computing the first few terms of ${b}_{n}$ we get\n\n$$\n3,\\,5,\\,7,\\,9,\\,…\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since ${a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534550 true] [17592186100840 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294768 13194139534550 true] [17592186100848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294559 13194139534550 true] [17592186110591 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294510 13194139534550 true] [17592186119887 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294667 13194139534550 true] [17592186124415 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294462 13194139534550 true] [17592186124431 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294552 13194139534550 true] [17592186124476 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294587 13194139534550 true] [17592186124495 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294741 13194139534550 true] [17592186129112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294788 13194139534550 true] [17592186129204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294566 13194139534550 true] [17592186129223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294594 13194139534550 true] [17592186129242 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294748 13194139534550 true] [17592186133686 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294755 13194139534550 true] [17592186133800 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294434 13194139534550 true] [17592186147378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294775 13194139534550 true] [17592186147450 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294734 13194139534550 true] [17592186147468 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294702 13194139534550 true] [17592186152151 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294455 13194139534550 true] [17592186152215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294605 13194139534550 true] [17592186152218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294545 13194139534550 true] [17592186157211 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294807 13194139534550 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294814 13194139534550 true] [17592186157302 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294821 13194139534550 true] [17592186166688 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294842 13194139534550 true] [17592186166696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294849 13194139534550 true] [17592186166788 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294476 13194139534550 true] [17592186166827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294448 13194139534550 true] [17592186166870 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294664 13194139534550 true] [17592186171947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294503 13194139534550 true] [17592186175908 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294441 13194139534550 true] [17592186179642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294517 13194139534550 true] [17592186179645 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294580 13194139534550 true] [17592186179705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294573 13194139534550 true] [17592186179738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294490 13194139534550 true] [17592186217189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294659 13194139534550 true] [17592186217198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294719 13194139534550 true] [17592186221344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294647 13194139534550 true] [17592186221351 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294650 13194139534550 true] [17592186221365 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294638 13194139534550 true] [17592186221376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294612 13194139534550 true] [17592186221379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294686 13194139534550 true] [17592186221401 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294631 13194139534550 true] [17592186229127 1020 #edb/ref 17592186294695 13194139534550 true] [17592186247199 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186247199 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" 13194139534550 false] [17592186257281 1017 #edb/ref 17592186257282 13194139534550 false] [17592186257281 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294497 13194139534550 true] [17592186257281 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\n- $8{y}^{′}-xy=3$\n- $3{y}^{″}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186257281 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\nII. $8{y}^{'}-xy=3$\nIII. $3{y}^{''}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1$" 13194139534550 false] [17592186257281 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{'}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{''}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{'}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534550 false] [17592186257281 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534550 true] [17592186260488 1017 #edb/ref 17592186260489 13194139534550 false] [17592186260488 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294762 13194139534550 true] [17592186260488 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186260488 1100 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\nII. - $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\nIII. - $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" 13194139534550 false] [17592186261365 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274941 13194139534550 false] [17592186261365 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294782 13194139534550 true] [17592186261365 1145 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA\\approx C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$" 13194139534550 false] [17592186261365 1145 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=C(t)$ over $[0,15].$ In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈C(3)Δ{t}_{1}+C(7)Δ{t}_{2}+C(12)Δ{t}_{3}+C(15)Δ{t}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $C(t)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12+12+25+6 \\\\  & =55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of customers served over a period of $15$ minutes. So, the total number of customers is approximately\n$55.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186261546 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274979 13194139534550 false] [17592186261546 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294795 13194139534550 true] [17592186261546 1145 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA\\approx f(2)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(8)Δ{x}_{3}+f(10)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx 5\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+12+4+2 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534550 false] [17592186261546 1145 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(2)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(8)Δ{x}_{3}+f(10)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈5\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+12+4+2 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186261599 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274994 13194139534550 false] [17592186261599 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294801 13194139534550 true] [17592186294238 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/8197d1111bbd4c3b8908bc12cbc58561ffd23fe06d9f9fa99f9ab815ed78733f.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534550 true] [17592186294238 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/8197d1111bbd4c3b8908bc12cbc58561ffd23fe06d9f9fa99f9ab815ed78733f.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534550 false] [17592186294238 1145 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/ba34bf96662682ec433c3ade0bf3f118638212101a0729fe95bbae05aee8bf1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294238 1145 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/ba34bf96662682ec433c3ade0bf3f118638212101a0729fe95bbae05aee8bf1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534550 false] [17592186294434 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294435 13194139534550 true] [17592186294434 1089 "q-105668" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294434 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294434 1100 "Given that $F(x,y,z)=3{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294434 1113 #uuid "bb2dffd2-9038-550e-ac80-acf29b387b94" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294434 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-6z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =6z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294436 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294437 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294438 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294439 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294440 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294438 13194139534550 true] [17592186294435 1110 #uuid "a1b48c20-cda5-5a3d-88b9-877ca4dcbec0" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294436 1034 "-6z\\,j+2\\,\\text{k}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294436 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294436 1111 #uuid "5d283f95-54bf-5543-a3e0-0b16ed1817c8" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294437 1034 "2\\,i" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294437 1111 #uuid "3e7270e5-fb82-52ce-8533-fca2f9a580dd" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294438 1034 "6z\\,j" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294438 1111 #uuid "c775e906-d35f-59ca-adb9-303e359d87d1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294439 1034 "0" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294439 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294439 1111 #uuid "28d7e6ed-54b5-5aa5-a4c8-6f7f98fdecd3" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294440 1034 "-6z\\,k" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294440 1111 #uuid "f26d3808-820d-5590-9bfd-c4b888b3180b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294441 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294442 13194139534550 true] [17592186294441 1089 "q-108854" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294441 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534550 true] [17592186294441 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x+2y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=4y-C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294441 1113 #uuid "8958083b-a553-5f14-95dd-a92020e3a321" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294441 1145 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2(2x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{1}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4z+1}{2z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z}{4z+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{2z}{4z+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{4z+1})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(z-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4z+1|) & =x+K \\\\ 4z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4z+1| & =8x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+4y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|8x+4y+1| & =8x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|8x+4y+1| & =4y-C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=8x+4y+1.\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294443 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294444 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294445 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294446 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294447 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294445 13194139534550 true] [17592186294442 1110 #uuid "e7286209-167d-5b5a-ad65-074a786bb7ce" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294443 1034 "2x+y" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294443 1111 #uuid "bf699cd1-bfd0-5245-9ea8-888ffc96b530" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294444 1034 "4x+2y-1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294444 1111 #uuid "27d00506-2c0f-5038-aac9-80cf9df1396d" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294445 1034 "8x+4y+1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294445 1111 #uuid "34c38378-410c-51e9-95c5-b83c4b50b95e" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294446 1034 "2x+y+3" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294446 1111 #uuid "6ba6ab6b-9f31-5a3d-862c-c80e5fbf2428" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294447 1034 "8x+4y" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294447 1111 #uuid "3b3265c5-f5e4-5646-8b71-f7d726decf73" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294448 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294449 13194139534550 true] [17592186294448 1089 "q-109693" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294448 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294448 1100 "The second-order differential equation $2{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-3y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294448 1113 #uuid "c344a893-9b6e-5aef-b629-9ed9f81985a3" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294448 1145 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{2}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294451 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294452 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294449 1110 #uuid "cedfb63c-47b8-5191-8c0c-989b4150708f" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294450 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294450 1111 #uuid "a8b54efa-3e3a-5412-90b6-6e5914044640" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294451 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294451 1111 #uuid "d1d646d5-85b9-536c-b336-eafaf91775d9" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294452 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294452 1111 #uuid "921e4fd5-c92d-5ad4-900f-c21751184697" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294453 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294453 1111 #uuid "7fc1c354-b8af-5711-a9ac-717a3743db85" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294454 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294454 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294454 1111 #uuid "f9489b65-16a1-5a34-8576-4b38bc1a20f5" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294455 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294456 13194139534550 true] [17592186294455 1089 "q-112252" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294455 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534550 true] [17592186294455 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294455 1113 #uuid "bfeef447-2f4a-59d5-bfbe-61062af1db4b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294455 1145 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294456 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294457 13194139534550 true] [17592186294456 