 unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294607 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294608 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294609 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294610 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294611 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294609 13194139534550 true] [17592186294606 1110 #uuid "894f6516-6630-5a0b-ad6a-d1eb445a7a2b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294607 1034 "u(2-t)" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294607 1111 #uuid "c6fe95c1-d553-5b34-8f36-da65baaf654b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294608 1034 "2u(-2-t)" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294608 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294608 1111 #uuid "fb2953aa-0138-5f3e-830b-aebdd9565b1f" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294609 1034 "2u(2-t)" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294609 1111 #uuid "9fa3bb70-d273-5e43-ac34-cd953118bf2f" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294610 1034 "2u(t-2)" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294610 1111 #uuid "a79e0c5b-de9f-53eb-8b18-c83d3b59c1f4" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294611 1034 "2u(2-t)+1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294611 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294611 1111 #uuid "749b99ba-8968-5374-85dd-cbed1b1e9f91" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294613 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294618 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294623 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294627 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1089 "q-331821" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1100 "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{b(t)}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{t}b(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-t}b(t)\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1113 #uuid "276a712f-2260-5901-9134-cf67e08a10a4" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294612 1145 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{t}b(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-1)t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $B(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-1)t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t=B(s-1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-1>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+1.\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294614 13194139534550 true] [17592186294613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294615 13194139534550 true] [17592186294613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294616 13194139534550 true] [17592186294613 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294617 131941395345