3t)" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294658 1111 #uuid "cb5bb032-f45f-5306-95d1-1b7f25da96b5" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294659 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294660 13194139534550 true] [17592186294659 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294662 13194139534550 true] [17592186294659 1089 "q-336300" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294659 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294659 1100 "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(3.6\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294659 1113 #uuid "386b24bd-3eff-5335-834a-d78cada3240c" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294659 1145 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (2.8\\times {10}^{6})}{3.6\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 4.5\\cdot 2.8}{3.6}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =23.345\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{23.345\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{23.345}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.8316…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.8\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294661 13194139534550 true] [17592186294660 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534550 true] [17592186294660 1041 "field-1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294660 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294661 13194139534550 true] [17592186294660 1110 #uuid "cfb1e79f-7789-5ee9-8707-8979eaf696ae" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294661 1034 "4.8" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294661 1111 #uuid "ece31cc5-4493-571f-be4f-43a27342195e" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294662 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294663 13194139534550 true] [17592186294662 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534550 true] [17592186294662 1041 "field-2" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294662 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294663 13194139534550 true] [17592186294662 1110 #uuid "7c91fc5c-380e-5e71-92bf-b258b18e1dfa" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294663 1034 "10^{2}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294663 1111 #uuid "0c18ce72-f6b8-5ce2-bf10-d7edc42cee9c" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294664 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294665 13194139534550 true] [17592186294664 1089 "q-337287" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294664 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294664 1100 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | $30t-26$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294664 1113 #uuid "ea828402-747f-5569-b98b-87a511fbc72e" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294664 1145 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot 3{e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})\\cdot (-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}27(t-1)+(3t+1) \\\\ 27(t-1)+3(3t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}30t-26 \\\\ 36t-24\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294665 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294666 13194139534550 true] [17592186294665 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534550 true] [17592186294665 1041 "field-1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294665 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294666 13194139534550 true] [17592186294665 1110 #uuid "339b992a-3d29-58ca-bd8e-a724ad5e6549" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294666 1034 "36t-24" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294666 1111 #uuid "30f6671d-6686-51cf-9fd2-69e21ded16da" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294667 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294668 13194139534550 true] [17592186294667 1089 "q-337570" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294667 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294667 1100 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." 13194139534550 true] [17592186294667 1113 #uuid "494759ac-e110-5f70-93f7-3b6af7555034" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294667 1145 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294669 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294670 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294671 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294672 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294673 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294673 13194139534550 true] [17592186294668 1110 #uuid "5bca0655-c64d-5fdc-9b72-2e0dc772b824" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294669 1034 "x=Ky" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294669 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294669 1111 #uuid "a6e8a51f-03c5-59c0-b027-d554459af78c" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294670 1034 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294670 1111 #uuid "712138d7-e620-5be5-b342-8a3843ff1257" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294671 1034 "y=Kx" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294671 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294671 1111 #uuid "88282ac6-c4c6-50a3-a829-eed1fc5efb79" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294672 1034 "xy=K" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294672 1111 #uuid "a0f73c9d-7bbc-5af3-8bdc-37f401b89654" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294673 1034 "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294673 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294673 1111 #uuid "8a6daef7-cda8-562b-b48f-730cd7e06337" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294674 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294675 13194139534550 true] [17592186294674 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294680 13194139534550 true] [17592186294674 1089 "q-339145" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294674 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534550 true] [17592186294674 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{6}y=x,\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139534550 true] [17592186294674 1113 #uuid "b18cb65d-b781-5075-91bf-6c656ac3ed30" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294674 1145 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $Q(x)=x.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (x/6)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}/12}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{6}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\cdot x \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{6}{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\cdot y & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}/12} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}) & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12})\\text{d}x & =\\int x{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12} & =\\int 6{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12} & =6{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12} & =6{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}+C \\\\ y & =6+C{e}^{-{x}^{2}/12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =6+C{e}^{-{0}^{2}/12} \\\\ 5 & =6+C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6-{e}^{-{x}^{2}/12}.\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294676 13194139534550 true] [17592186294675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294677 13194139534550 true] [17592186294675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294678 13194139534550 true] [17592186294675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294679 13194139534550 true] [17592186294675 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139534550 true] [17592186294675 1041 "field-1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294675 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294679 13194139534550 true] [17592186294675 1110 #uuid "4b8ce280-64e4-5b33-aca1-5b011968bfff" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294676 1034 "{e}^{12x}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294676 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294676 1111 #uuid "2fda18aa-1a14-51d2-8bcc-7f9ebda84856" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294677 1034 "\\frac{1}{x}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294677 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294677 1111 #uuid "5597871a-edaf-5f10-ba47-78d49a5116c2" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294678 1034 "{x}^{6}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294678 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294678 1111 #uuid "553c56bb-0776-5a1f-b5d2-9a856c8091b2" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294679 1034 "{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294679 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294679 1111 #uuid "1d12cb43-99f4-5b54-bd11-9c5ed6be9073" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294681 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294682 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294683 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294684 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294685 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1041 "field-2" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294685 13194139534550 true] [17592186294680 1110 #uuid "3333afe3-58ca-50c1-b6a5-292fad2e514a" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294681 1034 "6-{e}^{{x}^{2}}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294681 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294681 1111 #uuid "45b7b187-72c2-558b-b7d8-483c8c41df13" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294682 1034 "6x+5" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294682 1111 #uuid "b2a3b009-67bb-5628-bb7b-390289527147" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294683 1034 "{e}^{6{x}^{2}}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294683 1111 #uuid "54b038d8-eb0d-5f7a-badd-7ee99b7ac1b4" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294684 1034 "6{x}^{2}+5" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294684 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294684 1111 #uuid "91ca2193-058c-5514-989f-b0f6d9e74e61" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294685 1034 "6-{e}^{-{x}^{2}/12}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294685 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294685 1111 #uuid "8be3aed3-7040-5b8d-9741-df687946852a" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294687 13194139534550 true] [17592186294686 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294691 13194139534550 true] [17592186294686 1089 "q-339858" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294686 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294686 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\n-3.11\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294686 1113 #uuid "78ec1cb4-e132-5dea-aae3-8eefb04366b5" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294686 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =0+1+3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y \\\\  & =1+2+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈1.00,\\,y(2)≈6.00.\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294687 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294688 13194139534550 true] [17592186294687 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294689 13194139534550 true] [17592186294687 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294690 13194139534550 true] [17592186294687 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139534550 true] [17592186294687 1041 "field-1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294687 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294689 13194139534550 true] [17592186294687 1110 #uuid "7209cf16-57d4-5900-8bd8-54485eaf5c39" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294688 1034 "2.00" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294688 1111 #uuid "f3d88353-254b-5804-9765-3f06c5094051" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294689 1034 "1.00" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294689 1111 #uuid "fe399233-0217-54a1-aa3c-a7ec1bd9c0e9" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294690 1034 "-3.11" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294690 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294690 1111 #uuid "3d6c989d-d804-5d22-b9a8-daa58cbc1d8d" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294692 13194139534550 true] [17592186294691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294693 13194139534550 true] [17592186294691 