a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294734 1113 #uuid "65e9e479-9d60-5680-98f9-c749980f26f5" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294734 1145 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 6x{y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\ (8x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(8{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -3)(2\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4}.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294736 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294737 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294738 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294739 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294740 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294739 13194139534550 true] [17592186294735 1110 #uuid "ff76c0e3-6a2d-5899-9b89-f3e5c6c7a034" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294736 1034 "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-3/4}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294736 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294736 1111 #uuid "1cbcce0e-1454-5d89-9350-b17436e034d4" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294737 1034 "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294737 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294737 1111 #uuid "4c588564-4afe-5a5d-9f80-ea7d37b59d0f" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294738 1034 "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/8}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294738 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294738 1111 #uuid "8dfc6306-9f63-5860-aab3-fe71326b28b5" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294739 1034 "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294739 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294739 1111 #uuid "b50657bf-178f-5cc9-9895-91c3fbcee1b4" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294740 1034 "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/4}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294740 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294740 1111 #uuid "6bb9c97c-09f1-5af2-b11a-05d43c6dc34b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294741 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294742 13194139534550 true] [17592186294741 1089 "q-36978" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294741 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534550 true] [17592186294741 1100 "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,i+2yz\\,j+{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294741 1113 #uuid "f48dae94-b20d-5d85-9a71-53e264ee506a" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294741 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2yz)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C.\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294743 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294744 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294745 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294746 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294747 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294745 13194139534550 true] [17592186294742 1110 #uuid "60c5e6c4-7dc7-56e4-97c0-75ffef545d48" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294743 1034 "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+y{z}^{2}