550 true] [17592186294767 1111 #uuid "430bfad4-077d-5435-8e5c-52a43682396e" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294768 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294769 13194139534550 true] [17592186294768 1089 "q-50971" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294768 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534550 true] [17592186294768 1100 "Which of the following series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294768 1113 #uuid "8eacbe07-617c-5aa0-b112-80a6126a95e8" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294768 1145 "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n}{n+2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series I converges." 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294770 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294771 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294772 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294773 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294774 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294771 13194139534550 true] [17592186294769 1110 #uuid "e069c1e2-1f88-539a-bdd3-7b48fe255178" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294770 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294770 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294770 1111 #uuid "bdc0b9b6-ec61-5df4-8c46-693e7d0427b8" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294771 1034 "I only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294771 1111 #uuid "38af0f46-7210-5b2f-9ab7-741d9c22b506" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294772 1034 "III only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294772 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294772 1111 #uuid "79c2d597-5804-59e4-be59-8f9c4e04eb0b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294773 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294773 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294773 1111 #uuid "6690f368-afd8-5