e] [17592186294807 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294807 1100 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294807 1113 #uuid "694ae25e-76dc-5254-b28a-52858f45659d" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294807 1145 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}+\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{10}}=\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294809 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294810 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294811 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294812 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294813 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294813 13194139534550 true] [17592186294808 1110 #uuid "3b1e9640-f0e5-54fd-a3ed-9808ca7fd7ec" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294809 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294809 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294809 1111 #uuid "65a1ba53-d9ca-5ff1-9f3d-9c4fc0989396" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294810 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294810 1111 #uuid "dfbe921f-67d0-5706-a5c6-36b025c7070d" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294811 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294811 1111 #uuid "35cf3d11-b2c5-5e9f-acba-db3eb0fbcd21" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294812 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294812 1111 #uuid "0c319097-9c82-5855-844a-1c3b6a816381" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294813 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294813 1111 #uuid "93dc7b26-4f82-599a-a051-8216bdcf5712" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294814 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294815 13194139534550 true] [17592186294814 1089 "q-88041" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294814 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534550 true] [17592186294814 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+5.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294814 1113 #uuid "f8b94d42-fdad-56dc-8ffb-85fae3ce76d9" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294814 1145 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} & \\le {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+5} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)} & \\le {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}\\cdot {e}^{5} \\\\ 3x & \\le (x-1){e}^{5} \\\\ 3x & \\le {e}^{5}x-{e}^{5} \\\\ {e}^{5} & \\le ({e}^{5}-3)x \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>0$ and $x>1,$ which can be expressed as $(0,\\infty )$ and $(1,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=[\\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3}.$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294816 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294817 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294818 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294819 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294820 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294820 13194139534550 true] [17592186294815 1110 #uuid "a1f9e131-40c3-5990-bae0-dd5db678e4d9" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294816 1034 "no solution" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294816 1111 #uuid "2284eb37-4384-578f-a5ef-47f0d8f8908c" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294817 1034 "0\\le x\\le \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294817 1111 #uuid "56977187-a825-5d8e-90ef-5fd961c3163b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294818 1034 "1<x\\le \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294818 1111 #uuid "d51e9349-8b90-547d-b6c3-7e0a0cc80672" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294819 1034 "x>1" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294819 1111 #uuid "83498a57-74aa-53b0-9ba0-2fcac0574304" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294820 1034 "x\\ge \\frac{{e}^{5}}{{e}^{5}-3}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294820 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294820 1111 #uuid "6fecbb4c-e7dc-56ce-aff0-eb5bc102029b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294821 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294822 13194139534550 true] [17592186294821 1089 "q-89885" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294821 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534550 true] [17592186294821 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294821 1113 #uuid "a38e97ba-c156-5496-ad06-15f48100971c" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294821 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-1,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =1+(x+y) \\\\  & =x+y+1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y+1)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(-1,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294823 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294824 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294825 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294826 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294827 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294826 13194139534550 true] [17592186294822 1110 #uuid "482c157c-a744-5559-9cdf-215aac443a0f" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294823 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294823 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294823 1111 #uuid "c63c6329-411c-5ee5-a6fb-c199983b9670" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294824 1034 "I only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294824 1111 #uuid "84fccc02-cfba-59ba-965b-cf1df9b5fdd0" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294825 1034 "III only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294825 1111 #uuid "517f550c-47fc-55d5-aaff-7f19a5e4f9a8" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294826 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294826 1111 #uuid "69f32dbb-5860-546d-951c-b71903cf942b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294827 1034 "II only" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294827 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534550 true] [17592186294827 1111 #uuid "772c5e5c-a173-5bff-9c9f-6280a74d301e" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294828 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294829 13194139534550 true] [17592186294828 1089 "q-90693" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294828 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294828 1100 "Use the transformation $y=v(x)x$ to find the general solution of the following homogeneous differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294828 1113 #uuid "06efe7ad-4702-5be5-a0d4-8b7bf053efc8" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294828 1145 "With $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+(vx)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x(1+v)}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our solution, we finally obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294830 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294831 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294832 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294833 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294834 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1041 "selection" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294833 13194139534550 true] [17592186294829 1110 #uuid "bf385671-f58b-559d-b2cc-f06901fe762b" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294830 1034 "y=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C{x}^{2}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294830 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294830 1111 #uuid "73d46a52-c720-5be4-bc0d-36ed1a930c5e" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294831 1034 "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294831 1111 #uuid "9b167bc2-b2b5-58dd-9241-fa6cd6b3ff0e" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294832 1034 "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C{x}^{2}" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294832 1111 #uuid "92e86c9b-2d2e-5779-9135-add724e60455" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294833 1034 "y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+Cx" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294833 1111 #uuid "b5013620-85cf-596c-a1fc-5a58e3f4a66a" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294834 1034 "y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534550 true] [17592186294834 1111 #uuid "941c8fcd-64af-5b8b-af8b-f334e03514b0" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294835 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294836 13194139534550 true] [17592186294835 1089 "q-90806" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294835 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534550 true] [17592186294835 1100 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+20y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294835 1113 #uuid "3b23b2eb-59fb-5302-951a-3f3e5716ee74" 13194139534550 true] [17592186294835 1145 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+20{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(-2+4\\text{i})x})={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(-2+4\\text{i})x})={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4{e}^{-2x}+{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is not a solution." 13194139534550 true] [17592186294836 1000 #edb/ref 17592186